id: decisions/025-formalisation-spend
title: "Light until it can cause an effect, full once it can"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'formalisation-spend'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: formalisation-spend
axis_gloss: "whether a concept is given a full formal apparatus before use, or the smallest commitment that lets work proceed"
poles:
  a: minimum-sufficient-commitment
  b: heavy-specification
split: leaning
draft_status: full
leverage: 11.8
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(14) x split_weight(0.7) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.2)"

question: >
  Before a concept is used in a record, how much formal apparatus does it need —
  the smallest commitment that lets the next move be checked, or the full field
  set, schema, provenance attributes and tie-break ladder — and what decides
  which a record gets?

why_it_matters: >
  The corpus spends 275 lines on a loopback-only art pipeline no user can reach
  and four rules with no proof on the public capability tags, ships six
  mandatory provenance attributes on relations whose eight types are never
  defined, and leaves ten primitive vocabularies as empty key skeletons — and no
  record says why one concept got the full apparatus and its sibling got a
  sketch. Once answered, every bot below Dave knows how much to write before it
  acts, and a child Yawn copying a template knows whether it is copying the norm
  or the exception.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    3 judgments sit at minimum-sufficient-commitment (0 authored) and 11 at
    heavy-specification (1 authored). The repository asserts both directions on
    this axis.
  attributed_to: JUDGMENTS.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether Dave regards the braid's empty vocabularies as deliberate scaffolding
    to be filled by use or as premature specification; and whether the loopback
    art pipeline is expected to reach production, which would justify its weight
    under the carve-out.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: Minimum sufficient commitment. A concept enters a record with the fewest
      fields that let its claim be checked — a name, an owner, a proof condition.
      Fields, invariants, schemas and tie-break stages are added only when a test,
      receipt or replay needs them. Empty key skeletons are not shipped; an
      unweighted score is acceptable until a receipt shows the weights matter.
    cost: "Fill or remove ten empty vocabulary blocks in references/RELATIONSHIP_FIRST_RESEARCH_BRAID.yawn; decide which of the twenty-four per-turn provenance fields in interface/new-yawn-v0.1.yawn a receipt actually reads; state in templates/node.yawn that a new field needs a named consumer."
    risk: "The records that can spend or publish are held to the same light standard as a note, and the interception governance that today is test-enforced would have been 'added later'."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "A test that no tracked template or reference carries a top-level key whose value is an empty map or list skeleton with no comment or lacuna line, seeded with a shrink-only ledger from the braid's ten blocks; a line in templates/node.yawn requiring every template field to name a test, receipt or View that reads it."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0117"
      - "J0473"
      - "J1043"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: Heavy specification. Every primitive is fully specified before use —
      full field set, provenance attributes on every relation, a schema in schemas/,
      a deterministic tie-break — so downstream records cannot drift. Q-Space's
      shape (schemaVersion, protocolRevision, named object types, required fields on
      every object, dedicated templates) is the norm; question packets get a schema;
      the capability tags get invariants and a proof block.
    cost: "A schema and test for question packets; invariants and proof for interface/yawn-capability-tags-v1.yawn; definitions for the eight lateral relation types; a filled instance for each of the braid's ten empty vocabularies; the next-move score gets calibrated weights nobody has data for."
    risk: "Specification outruns use — the braid already declares ten vocabularies with no worked instance — and 'small moves over grand claims' in core/design-principles.yawn is contradicted by the tree's own habit."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "schemas/question-packet.v1.schema.json with a test; a test that every core/ primitive named in a schema has all its fields defined in prose; every interface/ contract carries invariants and a proof block."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0161"
      - "J0258"
      - "J0445"
      - "J0487"
      - "J0579"
      - "J0702"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: Minimum sufficient commitment is the default. carve-out, written down: a
      record that can spend, publish, mutate an external store, or be replayed as a
      receipt gets the full apparatus — schema, closed enums, provenance on every
      field, deterministic tie-break — because there the cost of drift is an effect.
      Everything else enters light and earns fields through receipts that read them.
      Where the heavy form already exists (q-space, local-observation-art,
      orientation-map, execution-relationship) it stays and names the effect that
      justifies it; a heavy record that reaches no effect (the 275-line loopback
      pipeline) says so in its own lacuna. An empty key skeleton is either filled
      with one worked instance or replaced by a lacuna line.
    cost: "One line in core/design-principles.yawn naming the carve-out; a `justified_by:` effect or receipt reference on each schema-backed contract; fill or lacuna the braid's ten empty blocks; no new schemas."
    risk: "'Can cause an effect' is read generously and everything becomes heavy anyway; or the loopback pipeline keeps its 275 lines on the argument that it will reach production."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "A test that every schemas/*.schema.json is named by a record of kind receipt, contract or automation that declares an effect or a replay, and that every empty skeleton block in references/ and templates/ is filled or carries a lacuna line, with a shrink-only ledger; keep tests/interception-statement-image-governance.test.mjs as the exemplar of full apparatus at the effect boundary."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0117"
      - "J0473"
      - "J1043"
      - "J0161"
      - "J0258"
      - "J0702"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.65
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    This one is close because almost nothing here is authored: one authored
    judgment on the heavy side (J0161, time as a fifteen-field primitive, and it
    argues its case) and none on the light side, so the axis is mostly the
    artifacts disagreeing with each other about proportion. Two things break the
    tie. First, the authored principle 'small moves over grand claims' in
    core/design-principles.yawn sits with A even though the inventory did not file
    it here. Second, GROUND_TRUTH fact 17(a) shows where the repository actually
    spends its heaviest apparatus: the only automation that can generate images,
    spend money or publish is the only one deliberately unscheduled and the one
    with a governance test — full apparatus exactly at the effect boundary, light
    elsewhere. That is C, already practised and never written. The revealed heavy
    cases the inventory flagged (275 lines for a loopback pipeline, twenty-four
    provenance fields per turn, ten empty skeletons) are the cost of not saying
    so. Confidence is moderate; Dave may regard the braid's skeletons as
    deliberate scaffolding.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "core/design-principles.yawn states the carve-out: full apparatus where a record can spend, publish, mutate or be replayed as a receipt; light elsewhere."
    - "Every schema in schemas/ is named by a record that declares the effect or replay justifying it, and a test in tests/ checks the link."
    - "references/RELATIONSHIP_FIRST_RESEARCH_BRAID.yawn carries either one worked instance or a lacuna line for each of its ten empty vocabulary blocks, and a test with a shrink-only ledger rejects new empty skeletons."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A new reference ships empty key skeletons with no lacuna, or a new
    effect-capable contract ships with four rules and no proof block, and npm test
    stays green.
  status: proposed

resolves:
  - "J0117"
  - "J0473"
  - "J1043"
  - "J0161"
  - "J0258"
  - "J0445"
  - "J0487"
  - "J0579"
  - "J0702"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
